 \ReviewerComment{c. A very important large-scale variable representing the mid-tropospheric flow (geopotential height at 500 hPa, z500) is missing. Additionally, Total-column water vapor (tcwv) is missing. As precipitation is closely linked to wind speed, why not compute wind speed from u and v components and use it as a predictor?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for these suggestions. We tested all three variables using saved refits of XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention that keep the original data split, input window, hyperparameters, training-only scaling, five seeds and test targets; no model was retuned. Wind speed was calculated as $\sqrt{u_{10}^2+v_{10}^2}$ at each grid cell before averaging over the study domain. Adding it to the original seven predictors produced no clear RMSE improvement for either model. Jointly adding the local 500-hPa geopotential (in m$^2$\,s$^{-2}$) and tcwv reduced BiLSTM-Attention RMSE by 1.18\%, with seed-based and moving-block intervals both excluding zero, but produced no clear change for XGBoost. Because the two variables were added together, this result cannot be attributed to either one individually. Neither variable was part of the original ERA5 extract, and these results indicate that the original candidate pool excluded potentially useful atmospheric information. We have added the results to Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_selection_results} and Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r3c_local_additions}; the original predictor set and main results are unchanged. Upstream predictors, including z500 and tcwv averaged over a larger region west of the study domain, are addressed in our response to comment (d).}
 
